Herein, we introduce a simple strategy to achieve sensitive ratiometric fluorescence detection of water in organic solvents by encapsulating red-emitting Tris(2,2'-bipyridyl)ruthenium(II) \(\left(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\right)\) in the pores of a blue-emitting MOF, MIL-101(Al)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), which was denoted as \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) hereafter. \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) shows stable red fluorescence emission in both water and organic solvents. Its high chemical stability and photo-stability make it popular as ideal fluorescence reference.28-32 \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\), prepared from \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) and 2-aminoterephthalic acid (BDC\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)), is an ideal platform to build ratiometric fluorescence sensor because of its large pores to accommodate guest molecules.33-35 Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) has blue fluorescence itself and the frameworks are stable in water and other solvents.36 Fine adjustment of reaction conditions, selectivity of the precursor ratio and optimal excitation wavelength achieved the efficient blue \((465~\mathrm{nm})\) and red \((615~\mathrm{nm})\) dual-emission fluorescence from \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The intensity of blue fluorescence enhanced with increased water content in organic solvents, while the fluorescence of the guest molecules, \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\), kept stable. Thus, turn-on sensing of water content was achieved through the ratiometric fluorescence of the dual-emission under single excitation of \(300~\mathrm{nm}\) with \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) as probe.

The cage size of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) is suitable to hosting the guest molecule, \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and the leakage of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) is inhibited with stable encapsulation. Therefore, the ratiometric fluorescence shows high stability and reusability for the detection of water content. The efficiency of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) is validated with the determination of water content in ethanol with broad water response range \(\left(0{-}100\%\right)\), high sensitivity, and fast response (less than 1 min). The sensor is also confirmed by the determination of water content in \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) and methanol. Therefore, we report the first example that \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) is used for ratiometric fluorescence and turn-on sensing of water with high sensitivity, low detection limit, fast response, broad range, and high reusability.

# EXPERIMENTAL SECTIONS

Chemicals and materials. Tris(2,2-bipyridyl) dichloride ruthenium(II) hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{II})(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) was obtained from Sigma–Aldrich, Shanghai, China. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\right)\) was purchased from TCI Chemistry Co. Ltd, Shanghai, China. Aluminum chloride hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) was obtained from Fuchen Chemical Reagents Factory, Tianjin. China. \(N,N\)-Dimethylformamide (DMF), dimethyl sulfoxidewith (DMSO), absolute ethyl alcohol and absolute methanol were purchased from Concord Reagent Co., Tianjin, China. All the chemicals were obtained at least of analytical grade and used without further purification. Ultrapure water was prepared with an Aquapro system (18.25 MΩ cm).

Instrumentation and Characterization. UV-Vis absorption spectrum was recorded by a UV-2450-visible spectrophotometer, Shimadzu, Japan. The steady-state fluorescence experiments were performed on a FL-4600 Fluorescence Spectrometer, Hitachi, Japan, equipped with a plotter unit and a quartz cell (cmx1 cm). We use the function of \(3\mathrm{D}\) scan of the Fluorescence Spectrometer to get the \(3\mathrm{D}\) fluorescence spectra. Infrared spectra were obtained by Bruker TENSOR 27 Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed on a PTC-10ATG-DTA analyzer heated from \(20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a ramp rate of \(15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) under air. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images was recorded with TecnaiG2 F20, FEI Co. (America) operated at an accelerating voltage of \(200~\mathrm{kV}\). Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) images were recorded with JSM-7500F, Japan. XRD patterns were obtained by a \(\mathrm{D}_{1/\mathrm{max}}-2500\) diffractometer (Rigaku, Japan) using \(\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{\AA})\). The content of Al and Ru was measured by Inductively Coupled Plasma Atomic Emission Spectroscopy (ICP-AES), IRIS advantage, Thermo, USA. Zeta potential was obtained by Nano-ZS zeta potential analysis meter (Malvern, Britain). Gas chromatography (GC) was recorded with Thermo TRACE 1300 (Italy). \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherm was recorded with ASAP2020/Tristar 3000 surface area and pore analyzer at \(274~\mathrm{K}\).

Preparation of MIL and MIL-NH2. MIL and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was prepared with simple one-pot method by a little modification of the previous work for the preparation of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). Aluminum chloride hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) \((37.5~\mathrm{mg}, 0.15~\mathrm{mmol})\) was added to 1,4-terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}, 32.8~\mathrm{mg}, 0.28~\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) or 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}, 40.2~\mathrm{mg}, 0.22~\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) in DMF \((6.0~\mathrm{mL})\). The as-obtained mixture was transferred to a stainless steel Teflon-lined autoclave of \(30~\mathrm{mL}\) capacity. After sonicated for 5 min until fully dissolved, the mixture maintained at \(403~\mathrm{K}\) for \(72~\mathrm{h}\) under static conditions. After cooling to room temperature, the solution was removed by centrifugation and the material was washed three times with DMF and ethanol to remove the residual BDC or \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\). Finally, the samples were dried under room temperature.

Preparation of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). Tris(2,2'-bipyridyl) dichloride ruthenium(II) hexahydrate \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right]\) \((55.0~\mathrm{mg}, 0.02~\mathrm{mmol})\) was added to the mixture solution of aluminum chloride hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}, 37.5~\mathrm{mg}, 0.15~\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) and 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}, 36.0~\mathrm{mg}, 0.2~\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) in DMF \((6.0~\mathrm{mL})\). The mixture was transferred to a \(30~\mathrm{mL}\) stainless steel Teflon-lined autoclave. After sonicated for \(5~\mathrm{min}\), the mixture maintained at \(403~\mathrm{K}\) for \(72~\mathrm{h}\) under static condition. After cooling to room temperature, the solvent was removed by centrifugation and the product was washed three times with DMF and ethanol to remove the residual \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\). Finally, the samples were dried under room temperature.

UV-visible absorption, fluorescence excitation, and emission spectra of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). For ultraviolet-
visible absorption spectra, the concentration of the free ligand, \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\), was \(0.025\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\) and other species were \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\). For the fluorescence excitation and emission spectra, the concentration of the species was \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\), and the slit was \(2.5 \times 5.0\) for free ligand \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\), and the others were set at \(5.0 \times 5.0\). The fluorescence stability of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was operated by incubating \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) in the mixture of water and ethanol, then soaked for 4 days. The fluorescence was detected everyday excited under \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\).

Ratiometric fluorescence detection of water. Typically, different volumes of water were added to ethanol in a clean quartz colorimetric tube to make a final water concentrations ranged from 0.05 to \(100\%\). Then, \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was added to keep the concentration of \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) in the mixture of methanol/water. These solutions were mixed well, and then, the luminescence spectra of the solution were recorded under an excitation wavelength of \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) after 10 s stirring.

Reusability of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) for water detection. \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was added into ethanol contained of \(10\%\) \((\nu/\nu)\) water. After detecting fluorescence response, the solution was removed by centrifugation and \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was collected after washed with ethanol. After dried under \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the recycled \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was used for detecting the mixture of ethanol and water \((50\%\,\nu/\nu)\). Using the same procedure, we detected the water content repeatedly for four cycles.

Analysis of real samples. Spirit samples obtained from local supermarket without further purification. We used the ratiometric fluorescence method to detect the water content in spirit samples. \(1.0\,\mathrm{mg\,Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was added to \(20\,\mathrm{mL}\) of the samples and the final sensor concentration was \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\). Then, ratiometric fluorescence was detected after 10 s stirring. We assumed that the spirit samples consisted of only water and ethanol. To validate the practicability of ratiometric fluorescence method, we detected the ethanol content by gas chromatography (GC) method with standard calibration curve strategy.

# RESULTS AND DISCUSSION

Preparation and characterization of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\). \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was prepared by simple one-pot method with a little modification of the previous work for the preparation of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). For stable fluorescence emission and high reproducibility, the guest molecules, \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\), should be embedded into \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in molecular level. We therefore optimized \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) content. The stable fluorescence intensity increased with molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) increased up to 1:10 (Figure S1), so 1:10 was selected as the optimal molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) to prepare \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\). Moreover, the content of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) did not remarkably affect the size and morphology of the products as shown in Figure S2. \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) exhibited spherical structure and uniform distribution with the average diameter of \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) as

illustrated with high resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, Figure 1A). \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) dispersed in water and ethanol easily to form transparent solution (Figure 1B). Fast diffusion kinetics were expected for fast response and high sensitivity of water detection by the nanosize and spherical structure of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\).

