The composition of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was tested with HRTEM elemental mapping and X-ray photoelectron (XPS) spectroscopy. Elemental mapping illustrated that aluminum, ruthenium, nitrogen, and oxygen elements were uniformly distributed in \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure 1C). Ruthenium and chlorine were observed in XPS spectroscopy of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure S3A). The fluorescence stability of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) was tested to validate the distribution of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\). Stable fluorescence was observed for four days (Figure S3B), and no leakage of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) was observed. \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\), with the size of \(11.5\,\mathrm{\AA}\), was efficiently encapsulated into \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\), whose pores were \(29\,\mathrm{\AA}\) and \(34\,\mathrm{\AA}\).

The experimental PXRD patterns of \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) were similar to that of simulated \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure S4A). The disappearance of \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) peak at \(7.5^{\circ}\) in \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) indicated that \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) dispersed in the pores of the host \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) but not mixture with \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in its crystalline form. Thus, \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) was embedded and distributed in the pores of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in molecular level rather than simple commixture. \(7.0\%\) wt \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) was found in \(\mathrm{Ru@MIL-NH}_{2}\) according to the result of inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectrometry (Table S1). Thanks to the excellent stability and fluorescence property, even little \(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}\) was effective enough to rise red emission to achieve ratiometric fluorescence. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed that
\(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) were stable up to \(413\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) after the loss of ethanol and DMF solvents (Figure S4B). \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) showed more weight loss than \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) because of the removal of pyridine group from \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\). Fourier transform infrared spectra of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) were recorded (Figure S4C). The band centered at \(1720\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) arisen from the stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) bond. The band at \(1595\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) from \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) stretching vibration was observed in \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\), but disappeared in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) possibly because it was merged in the strong adsorption of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{O-Al}\) bond at \(588\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) demonstrated the coordination of -COOH to \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\). Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was \(503.9\) and \(1328.5\,\mathrm{m}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively (Figure S4D). The lower BET area of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) indicated that \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) was encapsulated in the pores of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\).

Optical property of \(\mathbf{Ru}@\mathbf{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The UV-visible absorption spectra of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) were tested (Figure S5A). The absorption was observed at 290 and \(450\,\mathrm{nm}\) for \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and at \(345\,\mathrm{nm}\) for \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\). The absorption of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) at \(290\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(350\,\mathrm{nm}\) was consistent with the merge of \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\). Two excitation peaks were observed at \(260\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(380\,\mathrm{nm}\) for \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\), so the emission of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) originated from \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\). A broad excitation range was observed for \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) from 290 to \(500\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S5B). Thus, \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) integrated the optical behavior of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\).

The emission peak of \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) was observed at \(455\,\mathrm{nm}\) and that of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) at \(640\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S5C). \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) showed two emission peaks at \(465\) and \(615\,\mathrm{nm}\) with blue and red emissions. The emission of \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) red shifted to \(465\,\mathrm{nm}\) after the coordination between \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ions to form \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The blue-shift emission from \(640\) to \(615\,\mathrm{nm}\) illustrated that energy transfer occurs between \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). Thus, \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) was embedded into the MOFs at molecular level with easy energy transfer in terms of the high stability of fluorescence.

The rational selection of excitation wavelength was one of the merits of fluorescence detection for special application. To detect broad water content, the \(3\mathrm{D}\) emission profiles of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) were recorded in pure ethanol, pure water, and 1:1 mixture of water/ethanol. In pure ethanol, the emission from \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was weaker than that of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) encapsulated in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure 2A). However, the emission behavior reversed in pure water as blue fluorescence emission was higher than red one (Figure 2B). If \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) was selected as excitation wavelength for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in 1:1 mixture of water/ethanol, almost the same emission intensity was observed from \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) (Figure 2C). Moreover, at the excitation of

\(300\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in pure water and pure ethanol showed obviously blue and red emission at \(465\) and \(615\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively, as validated with the results in Figure 2D. Red and blue images of pure ethanol and water were clearly observed (inset in Figure 2D). Therefore, \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) was selected as single excitation wavelength and provided the possibility to detect water in ethanol with the range of \(0-100\%\) with ratiometric fluorescence of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\).

