Water detection with \(\mathbf{Ru}@\mathbf{MIL-NH}_{2}\) as probe. The dual emission of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was tested with different water content in ethanol to validate its efficiency. The emission at \(465\,\mathrm{nm}\) was enhanced gradually but the emission around \(615\,\mathrm{nm}\) kept stable relatively under single-excitation of \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) with water content increasing from \(0\) to \(100\%\) (Figure 3A). A good linear relationship was observed for the intensity ratio between the emissions at \(465\) and \(615\,\mathrm{nm}\) and the water content (Figure 3B). Moreover, the color change was easily identified by naked eyes under excited at \(302\,\mathrm{nm}\) (inset in Figure 3B). Trace water response was recorded from \(0\) to \(10\%\) \((\nu/\nu)\) to illustrate the sensitivity of the dual-emission probe and good linear response was observed (Figure S6A, B). A low detection limit was observed for water content in ethanol as \(0.02\%\) \((\nu/\nu)\), which is lower than that obtained from traditional Karl Fischer titration method. We also tested the effect of the dose of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The linear calibration curve still kept stable even with high dose of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure S6). Thus, the influence of probe concentration and environment interference could be eliminated as a merit of ratiometric fluorescence. The color change with increasing water content was illustrated with the CIE
