To illustrate the superiority of ratiometric fluorescence from \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\), we detected water content with \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) as probe as a comparison (Figure 3D). Increasing water content resulted in increased emission intensity, but only blue fluorescence was observed and the intensity change was difficult to distinguish by naked eyes (inset in Figure 3E). The coordinate points were difficult to distinguish to each other observed with \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) as probe (Figure 3F). Thus, we built a turn-on ratiometric fluorescence sensor with wide response range, low detection limit, and high sensitivity to detect water in ethanol.

To reveal the reusability, the PXRD patterns and ratiometric fluorescence profiles of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) responding to 10 and \(50~\%\) (v/v) water in ethanol were recorded repeatedly (Figure S7). After four cycles, the PXRD patterns and ratiometric fluorescence were still stable to illustrate the practical use of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure S7). We also explored the influence of ion strength for the fluorescence stability of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) with different concentration of \(\mathrm{NaCl}\). Even \(17~\mathrm{mM}\) of \(\mathrm{NaCl}\) was added, the fluorescence kept stable as observed in Figure S8. And the ratiometric fluorescence intensity was stable in different ion strength.

Water detection mechanism of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). In order to explore the water-detection mechanism, we detected the response for \(\mathrm{MIL}\) and \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) to water content. MIL did not response to water as its emission spec

tra observed in Figure S9A. The fluorescence emission intensity was related to water content when \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) (Figure S9B) and \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure 3D) were selected as probes. Well linear response was observed as shown in Figure S8C and Figure 3E. The results indicated that the ligand, \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\), is sensitive to water. Three \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) hybrid orbitals in \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) form three covalent bonds with two hydrogen atoms and the carbon atom in benzene ring. Correspondingly, a delocalized \(\pi\) bond, \(\pi_{\mathrm{u}}^{12}\), forms among benzene ring, two carbonyl groups, and the nitrogen atom in ethanol condition in ethanol (Figure 4A). Water showed stronger capacity than ethanol to protonate \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and the nitrogen atom in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) donated its lone-pair electron to \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)to form \(-\mathrm{NH}^{3+}\) with increasing water content in ethanol. \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) was protonated and the nitrogen in \(-\mathrm{NH}^{3+}\) became \(\mathrm{sp}^{3}\) hybrid, so the delocalized \(\pi_{\mathrm{u}}^{12}\) bond became \(\pi_{\mathrm{u}}^{10}\) around the benzene ring and two carbonyl groups in water condition. The protonation of nitrogen atom in \(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\) changed the energy of LUMO and HOMO of the \(\pi\)-conjugate system of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) to result in the emission change. The changed \(\pi\)-conjugate system of \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was validated with 3D fluorescence profiles in Figure 2. The emission was observed at \(440~\mathrm{nm}\) with the excitation at \(350\) and \(240~\mathrm{nm}\) in water (Figure 2B). However, the emission at \(350~\mathrm{nm}\) excitation became weak, while the other emission red-shifted to \(500~\mathrm{nm}\) with the excitation blue-shifted to \(200~\mathrm{nm}\) in ethanol (Figure 2A).
The protonation of nitrogen atom in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) was confirmed by the results of zeta potential test. \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) has lower zeta potential than \(\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in both water and ethanol (Figure S1o) as Cl ions exist on \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) because chlorine was observed in the XPS pattern of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) (Figure \(\mathrm{S}_{3}\mathrm{C}\)). The zeta potential of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in ethanol was obviously higher than in water. We considered that the counter ions Cl, on the surface, increased for the lower zeta potential after the protonation of nitrogen atoms in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\).

\(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) possesses more hydrophilic active sites such as aluminum cluster and free \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), so water molecules enter cages easily. Water displaced ethanol molecules and led to the formation of the hydrated form of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) with increasing water content (Figure 4B). The LUMO and HOMO of \(\pi\)-conjugate system of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) changed to result in the changed blue emission. The water content in organic solvents could be detected immediately after added \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) for 10 s stirring. Thus, the detection of water content is finished within one minute.

