Figure 4. Water-responding mechanism with \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) as probe. (A) The protonation of the nitrogen atom in amino group changed the \(\pi\)-conjugate system of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). Dash lines illustrate the big \(\pi\) bonds. (B) Trace of water was absorbed in the pores of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) to result in the turn-on blue fluorescence. (C) Fluorescence profile of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) in ethanol (EtOH), methanol (MeOH), \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide with (DMSO)/water mixture \((5.2\% \, \mathrm{v/v})\). (D) The photos of different solvents contained \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) with water content ranged from 10 to \(90\%\) (v/v) under the excitation of \(302 \, \mathrm{nm}\).

To validate the water exchange response mechanism, ratiometric fluorescence response of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) to the water content in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), N,N-dimethyl formamide (DMF), and methanol (methanol) was recorded (Figure 4C). The color change from red to blue was clearly observed by naked eyes as illustrated in Figure 4D. Similar to the water response in ethanol, water in the organic solvents protonated the amino groups of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and achieved the emission change obviously. Therefore, \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) is a general agent for the ratiometric fluorescence detection of water content in organic solvents by carefully selecting excitation wavelength at \(300 \, \mathrm{nm}\).

Detection of water content in real samples. The ratiometric fluorescence strategy was validated with the detection of water content in white spirit samples with the outstanding characteristics of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\). The water content in the samples was \(71.6\) and \(40.7\%\), respectively. To validate the precision of the proposed method, we detected the ethanol content with gas chromatography (GC) method as a comparison (Figure S1i). The relative error of the results between GC-method and the ratiometric fluorescence was \(1.6\) and \(2.0\%\), respectively (Table S2). Thus, the ratiometric fluorescence method has great potential to practical application.

To detect trace of water in organic solvents conveniently and quickly, we prepared fluorescence water-detection test strip by coating \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) on filter paper evenly to form paper-supported film. The testing solvents were added to the test strip to judge the water content rapidly by naked eyes with observable color changing. The red-emitting film turned to blue immediately during the water increase from 0 to \(5\%\) (Figure 5A). A linear relationship of CIE coordinates versus water content can be established clearly (Figure 5B), which served as a quantitative ratiometric fluorescence sensor for trace of water in solvents.

